<h2>The situation</h2>

<p>
  A portfolio of more than 110 client websites was running on legacy custom
  WordPress themes. The themes were increasingly expensive to maintain, slow,
  and difficult to extend. The business decision to move everything onto a
  modern block-based framework was straightforward. The SEO risk was not.
</p>

<p>
  These are multi-location healthcare practices. For most of them, organic
  search is the single largest source of new patient inquiries, and the
  conversion is a phone call or a booked appointment rather than an online
  purchase. A migration that quietly dropped a handful of ranking location
  pages would not show up as a traffic chart anomaly first &mdash; it would show
  up as a practice owner asking why the phone stopped ringing.
</p>

<h2>What made it genuinely hard</h2>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Scale with variation.</strong> 110+ sites sounds like one problem
    solved 110 times. It is not. Each site had accumulated its own URL quirks,
    orphaned pages, legacy campaign landing pages, and locally-added content
    that nobody had inventoried.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Templated structures hiding real differences.</strong> Sites that
    looked identical in the CMS had meaningfully different internal link graphs
    and indexation profiles once crawled.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Distributed stakeholders.</strong> Every site had a marketing
    manager and a practice owner who needed to understand what was changing and
    why, in terms that had nothing to do with crawl budget.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Tooling that stopped being supported mid-project.</strong> The
    multisite network relied on the Broadcast plugin by Edward Plainview to push
    content from a parent site to child sites. When the license for updates was
    not renewed, several of the workflows the migration depended on had to be
    replaced with something we owned and could maintain ourselves.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>The approach</h2>

<h3>1. Inventory before architecture</h3>
<p>
  Nothing moved until each site had a complete URL inventory reconciled across
  crawl data, Google Search Console performance data, and analytics. The
  reconciliation matters: a page with no internal links and no crawl depth can
  still be the top organic entry point for a specific market. Crawl-only
  inventories miss those; GSC-only inventories miss the pages that should be
  consolidated.
</p>

<h3>2. Information architecture driven by search demand</h3>
<p>
  The new framework was an opportunity to fix structures that had grown
  organically over years &mdash; service pages that competed with each other,
  location pages buried three levels deep, and thin content that diluted topical
  focus. I mapped the target IA against actual query demand per market rather
  than replicating the existing tree by default.
</p>

<h3>3. Redirect mapping as a managed artifact</h3>
<p>
  Redirects were treated as a reviewable deliverable, not a cleanup step.
  Pattern-level regex rules handled predictable structural changes at scale;
  one-to-one mappings handled the pages that carried real equity. Every map was
  validated before launch and re-validated after, with explicit checks for
  redirect chains, loops, and anything resolving to a soft 404.
</p>

<h3>4. Post-launch monitoring with a defined response</h3>
<p>
  Launch is the start of the measurement window, not the end of the project.
  Each migration had a monitoring period watching indexation coverage, query-level
  position changes, and conversion actions &mdash; with agreed thresholds for when
  a change warranted intervention versus when it was normal post-migration
  volatility.
</p>

<h2>The tooling I built to make it possible</h2>

<p>
  A migration at this scale is not limited by strategy &mdash; it is limited by
  how many hours the team can spend moving content by hand without introducing
  errors. Rather than absorb that cost, I built and tested a purpose-made
  toolkit for the project: Python scripts that work through the WordPress REST
  API, JavaScript bookmarklets for the tasks that live inside the WordPress
  admin, a custom WordPress plugin, a reusable Yoast settings template, and a
  set of AI assistants for recurring content work. I used AI as a development
  partner to build and test these tools quickly, then validated every one of
  them against real content before it touched a client site.
</p>

<p>
  Each tool is documented in an internal library so anyone on the team can pick
  it up without needing me in the room. That was a deliberate goal: tooling that
  only its author can run is a bottleneck, not a benefit.
</p>

<h3>Python scripts for content migration and cleanup</h3>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Broadcast Content and MW to Web 2.0 Broadcast Tool.</strong> These
    connect to a source and a destination WordPress site over the REST API and
    carry a post across intact &mdash; title, body content, featured image, and
    Yoast SEO metadata including the SEO title, meta description, and canonical.
    They handle custom post types, support broadcasting a single post or every
    post within a date range, and create the destination post as a draft so a
    human reviews it before anything goes live. This directly replaced the
    capability we lost when the Broadcast plugin stopped receiving updates.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Migrate Blogs.</strong> For legacy sites where the REST API was not
    available on the source, this script reads a list of post URLs, scrapes the
    title, article content, featured image, and Yoast metadata, uploads the
    image to the new media library, and creates the post on the destination
    site &mdash; logging every success and failure so nothing is silently lost.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Website Content Diff Checker.</strong> This one crawls a client site
    and the template site it was built from, compares the content page by page,
    and reports what is missing or out of sync. It caches the template crawl so
    repeat runs are fast. This became our pre-launch quality gate: it catches the
    page that quietly did not come across, which is exactly the failure mode that
    costs a practice its rankings.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Fix Blog URLs.</strong> Checks a list of blog URLs for 404s and, when
    it finds one, searches the site for the correct destination and writes the
    old and new URL pair to a CSV. That CSV feeds the redirect map directly,
    which turns the most tedious part of redirect work into a reviewable
    artifact.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Update Blog Titles/Descriptions and Update Blog Tags.</strong> Bulk
    updates driven by a reviewed CSV &mdash; titles and Yoast meta descriptions
    in one, taxonomy tags in the other, with tags created automatically when they
    do not yet exist. Both write a detailed log so every change is traceable.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Strip Empty Paragraphs, Deduplicate Topics, and Sync Blog Publish
    Dates.</strong> Small utilities that handle the unglamorous cleanup work:
    removing empty markup left behind by the old editor, deduplicating topic
    lists, and keeping publish dates consistent between source and destination so
    the content history stays accurate.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>JavaScript bookmarklets for the work inside WP Admin</h3>

<p>
  Some of this work could not be done over an API, because it lived in a plugin
  interface. Bookmarklets were the right answer there: dependency-free,
  instantly shareable with the team, and run right on the screen a person is
  already looking at.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Broadcast unlinker.</strong> Disconnecting child-blog articles from
    the parent site was a repetitive, multi-click task per post, repeated across
    a very large number of posts. This bookmarklet works through the post list
    automatically &mdash; opening each Broadcast modal, selecting the unlink
    option, submitting, and moving on to the next one &mdash; with a built-in
    guard that halts the run and reports to the console if the same post keeps
    reappearing, so it fails safely rather than looping.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Connected child site lister.</strong> Prints the full list of child
    sites connected to a given parent post to the browser console. Knowing
    exactly which sites a post reaches before changing anything removes the
    guesswork from every decision that follows.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Targeted blog deselection.</strong> Deselects connected blogs so a
    single site's version of a post can be updated on its own, without pushing
    the change out to every other site on the network. This made it possible to
    honor one practice's content request without affecting the other 100+.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Duplicate post title checker.</strong> Scans post titles and flags
    duplicates, which surfaced accidental duplicate posts on the parent site
    before they could be broadcast outward and multiply into a network-wide
    duplicate content problem.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Copy meta description to excerpt.</strong> A one-click fix for a
    recurring requirement in the new template, applied consistently rather than
    from memory.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>A custom WordPress plugin for metadata at scale</h3>

<p>
  Partway through the project, a Yoast SEO update changed the bulk editor in two
  ways that mattered to us. Entries were no longer listed in alphabetical order,
  and the table was split across multiple paginated pages. Individually those are
  minor interface choices. In combination, they broke a workflow we depend on.
</p>

<p>
  Our team maintains a Google Sheet of template SEO metadata with dynamic
  replacement variables for city names, provider types, and other
  practice-specific values. That sheet is only usable if the destination list is
  in a predictable alphabetical order on a single screen &mdash; otherwise every
  entry has to be matched by hand across several pages, which is slow and
  error-prone at portfolio scale.
</p>

<p>
  Rather than change our process to fit the plugin, I wrote a small WordPress
  plugin, <strong>Edit Yoast Fields</strong>, that restores the workflow. It
  registers the Yoast SEO title and meta description fields with the REST API,
  then provides a single admin screen with separate tabs for titles and
  descriptions. It requests content alphabetically by title, follows pagination
  behind the scenes, and presents everything on one page &mdash; showing the
  current value next to an editable new value for each entry, with per-row saving
  and a Save All option. It covers our full content model: posts, pages, events,
  hearing aids, locations, professionals, and testimonials. Permission checks
  follow WordPress conventions, so the plugin grants no access a user does not
  already have.
</p>

<p>
  The practical result is that applying a full set of templated metadata to a
  site went from a tedious multi-page reconciliation to a straightforward
  paste-and-review pass.
</p>

<h3>A reusable Yoast settings import template</h3>

<p>
  Yoast configuration is easy to get subtly wrong and tedious to verify across
  110+ sites. I built a settings generator that produces a ready-to-paste Yoast
  import file encoding our standard configuration &mdash; title and description
  patterns, and indexation rules that keep internal-only content types and
  taxonomies out of the XML sitemap and the index, such as attachments, design
  library collections, document and testimonial post types, and tag and format
  archives.
</p>

<p>
  This turned a long manual checklist into a single import step, which does more
  than save time: it guarantees every site in the portfolio starts from the same
  known-good baseline, so a sitemap full of pages nobody should be landing on
  never becomes a surprise after launch.
</p>

<h3>AI assistants for repeatable content tasks</h3>

<p>
  I also built a set of custom Gemini Gems (now called Skills) so the team could
  produce consistent, on-brand content for the tasks that recur on every
  migration &mdash; an <strong>Alt Text Generator</strong> for image
  accessibility, a <strong>Meta Description Generator</strong> tuned for blogs
  and evergreen pages, a <strong>Google Business Profile post generator</strong>
  that respects platform limits and best practices, a
  <strong>Category Picker</strong> that assigns blog categories from our approved
  list, a <strong>Website List Comparison Tool</strong> for reconciling site
  inventories between sources, and a <strong>Top Local Competitors Synthesis</strong>
  tool that identifies local competitors from raw data and explains its
  reasoning.
</p>

<p>
  Each one encodes our standards and guidelines so the output is consistent no
  matter who runs it. As with everything else here, the AI handles throughput and
  a person reviews before anything reaches a client site.
</p>

<h3>What the tooling actually bought us</h3>

<p>
  The honest value was not speed for its own sake &mdash; it was consistency and
  confidence. Scripted migration means the 90th site gets the same care as the
  first, the settings template means every site starts from the same known-good
  configuration, the diff checker means we find a missing page before the client
  does, and the logs mean that when someone asks what happened to a specific
  post, we can answer precisely rather than from recollection. That is what lets
  us tell a practice owner, truthfully, that their content came across intact.
</p>

<h2>What I'd tell another strategist</h2>

<p>
  The single highest-leverage habit in a migration of this size is
  <strong>refusing to treat the redirect map as a technical afterthought</strong>.
  It is the artifact that preserves years of accumulated equity, and it deserves
  the same review rigor as the design.
</p>

<p>
  The second is communication cadence. A migration that goes perfectly but
  surprises the stakeholder still damages trust. Telling a marketing manager
  in advance that they may see two to four weeks of ranking fluctuation turns a
  support escalation into an expected event.
</p>

<p>
  The third is a willingness to build. Vendor tools get discontinued, and vendor
  interfaces change in ways that quietly break a workflow that was running well.
  Both happened on this project. The choice in that moment is to absorb the
  manual cost indefinitely or to rebuild the capability. With AI as a development
  partner, building a tested, documented replacement is now a matter of days
  rather than weeks &mdash; and the version you build yourself fits your
  workflow, logs what you need it to log, and does not change out from under you
  on someone else's release schedule.
</p>
